From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mzak <mzak.amin@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 10:38:15 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] power: supply: add the MT5806 pen charger of the OnePlus Pad
 4

The pen of the OnePlus Pad 4 is charged by a Maxic MT5806 wireless
power transmitter. The IC finds the pen, negotiates and transfers the
power by itself; the limits of the transfer are part of its firmware.
The host has three jobs, done here as the vendor driver does them:

- The supply: the PMIC's hBoost converter belongs to the charger
  firmware. Its voltage is asked for through PMIC GLINK (owner 32785,
  opcode 0x10007): the idle value at start and after a pen is taken
  off, the charge value when one is attached. Nothing is switched on
  before the firmware has confirmed the idle value.
- Supervision: a transfer goes on only for a pen that has sent a valid
  address within 5 s of the attach. It is stopped when the pen reports
  a full battery or asks for a stop, when the IC ends it with an error
  (over-current, over- or under-voltage, foreign object, temperature)
  and after 120 minutes. After an error the IC is power cycled once
  the pen is gone.
- Reporting: "present", "state", the pen's reported charge and the
  live supply voltage and current as device attributes, change events
  with PEN_PRESENT, an "inhibit" switch for user space and "rx_soc" for
  the pen's battery level known from Bluetooth (a full pen that has
  dropped to 80 % is charged again). A status file in debugfs has the
  details and the last events.

Differences from the vendor driver, all on the careful side: the
firmware of the IC is never written (no update, no calibration), and
the transmitter is only used with a firmware version seen on this
tablet; supply voltages above the tablet's charge value are refused;
the transmitter stays off during the start sequence, while the supply
voltage is not confirmed, while the link to the charger firmware is
down and, if the interrupt cannot wake the system, during suspend.

Tested on the OnePlus Pad 4 without a pen (kernel q56, area-combo-49):
the IC answers (id 0x5806, firmware 0x20022, calibration as the
vendor's log), the charger firmware confirms the 7 V supply, the
control lines match the vendor kernel's idle state, inhibit switches
the transmitter off and on, and after an unbind the power line reads
low and the transmitter-off line high.

Signed-off-by: mzak <mzak.amin@gmail.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 drivers/power/supply/Kconfig              |   13 +
 drivers/power/supply/Makefile             |    1 +
 drivers/power/supply/mt5806_pen_charger.c | 1637 +++++++++++++++++++++
 3 files changed, 1651 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 drivers/power/supply/mt5806_pen_charger.c

diff --git a/drivers/power/supply/Kconfig b/drivers/power/supply/Kconfig
index b89ef40df7f8..e93dfe881843 100644
--- a/drivers/power/supply/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/power/supply/Kconfig
@@ -1191,6 +1191,19 @@ config FUEL_GAUGE_MM8013
 	  the state of charge, temperature, cycle count, actual and design
 	  capacity, etc.
 
+config CHARGER_MT5806_PEN
+	tristate "Maxic MT5806 pen charger (OnePlus Pad 4)"
+	depends on I2C
+	depends on GPIOLIB
+	depends on QCOM_PMIC_GLINK
+	help
+	  Say Y or M here for the wireless pen charger of the OnePlus Pad 4:
+	  a Maxic MT5806 power transmitter on the magnet side of the tablet,
+	  fed from the PMIC's hBoost converter through the charger firmware.
+	  The driver powers the IC, lets it charge a pen that has identified
+	  itself, stops on the pen's request, on errors and after two hours,
+	  and reports the pen to user space.
+
 config MACSMC_POWER
 	tristate "Apple SMC Battery and Power Driver"
 	depends on MFD_MACSMC
